does not support user-defined reductions for arrays easily. We can use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) with critical section to update global list directly. This preserves exact order if we process groups in original order (i.e., static scheduling with chunk=1). But dynamic scheduling reorders iterations; however, we could use ordered clause? That would serialize.\n\nBetter: Use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) over groups, but then each thread processes contiguous chunks. Within a thread, points are processed in increasing group index order (since static assigns consecutive groups to same thread). So the relative order of points within a thread is preserved. Across threads, we need to merge per-thread lists while respecting global group order for ties.\n\nWe can assign each point a unique key: (group_index * MAX_POINTS_PER_GROUP + j) but that requires knowing max length. Simpler: precompute cumulative lengths array cum_len[n_groups+1] where cum_len[0]=0, cum_len[i+1]=cum_len[i]+groups[i].length. Then global index for point (i,j) = cum_len[i] + j.\n\nThen each thread builds its local top-k list storing distance, label, and global_index. After parallel region, we collect all entries from all threads into a single array of size num_threads * k (since each thread may have up to k entries). Then sort this array by distance then global_index. Take first k elements.\n\nBut is it sufficient that each thread only keeps its own top-k? As argued above, for ties at boundary we might miss some equal-distance points with smaller indices from other threads. However, consider: Suppose two points A and B have same distance d, where A has index 10 (from group0) and B has index 20 (from group1). Sequential order processes A first, so it gets inserted into top-k list at position x if there is room or if d < distances[x]. If the global k-th smallest distance is also d, then after processing all points, the final list will include A before B. In parallel version with