m group0. In parallel: Thread0 local top-k includes [1,2,3,4,5]; thread1 local top-k includes [5] (since its only point). Merge and sort by distance then index gives [1,2,3,4,5] with both 5s? Actually we have two entries with distance 5: one from group0 (index small) and one from group1 (index larger). When sorting, the first five will be distances 1-4 and then the smallest-indexed 5. So final list includes only one 5 (the correct one). Good.\n\nNow consider scenario where a thread has many points with same distance d at boundary such that its local top-k excludes some of them due to index order within that thread, but those excluded ones have smaller global indices than other threads' candidates? Example: Thread0 processes groups in order; it encounters two points both with distance 5 (first point A index=10, second B index=11). k=1. Sequential will keep only the first encountered (A) because when processing B, distances[0]=5 and d==5 not less, so no insertion. So final list contains A. In parallel: Thread0 local top-1 includes A; thread1 may have a point C with distance 6. Merge gives [A]. Correct.\n\nBut what if there is another thread's point D with same distance 5 but smaller global index than B? That cannot happen because indices are assigned sequentially across all points in order of groups and within group order. So any point from earlier group has smaller index than later group. Within a group, earlier point has smaller index. So the ordering by index exactly matches sequential processing order.\n\nThus using per-thread local top-k (keeping only k entries) is sufficient for correctness because if a point is not in its thread's local top-k, then there are at least k points from that same thread with distances <= its distance and with smaller indices (since they were inserted earlier). Those points will be present in the global merge and will push out this point. So we don't need to keep all candidates.\n\nTherefore algorithm:\n1. Precompute cumulative length