g may cause load imbalance but we use schedule(dynamic) for variable-length groups. Could also try guided or static with chunk size 1.\n\nWe must ensure that the merge step is correct and deterministic. Sorting by distance then global_index guarantees exact match to sequential because sequential insertion order corresponds to increasing global index (since points are processed in order of i,j). For equal distances, smaller index comes first.\n\nNow we need to produce final source code for src/knn.c and possibly modify knn.h? We only change .c file. The header already has necessary includes; we may add #include <omp.h> in knn.c or knn.h. Better to include in knn.c before using omp functions.\n\nAlso need to handle that the number of threads is set by OMP_NUM_THREADS environment variable (4 cores). We'll use omp_get_max_threads() for allocation.\n\nEdge cases: k may be 0? Not likely but we can ignore. Also groups length could be zero? Possibly not.\n\nWe must preserve other files unchanged. Only modify src/knn.c and maybe add include in knn.h if needed, but better to keep changes minimal.\n\nNow write the code.\n\nImplementation details:\n- In knn function, compute cum_len array: int *cum = malloc((n_groups+1)*sizeof(int)); cum[0]=0; for i=0..n_groups-1: cum[i+1]=cum[i]+groups[i].length;\n- Get num_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n- Allocate shared arrays: float (*dist_local)[k] = malloc(num_threads * k * sizeof(float)); similarly char (*lab_local)[k]; int (*idx_local)[k];\n- Initialize each thread's row with sentinel values (e.g., distances to INFINITY or -1). We'll use a large number like 1e30f.\n- #pragma omp parallel\n   {\n       int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n       // initialize local arrays for this thread? Actually we can do it before parallel region in shared memory, but each thread should only touch its own row. Better to have each thread initialize its own row inside parallel region using a single master or each thread initializes its own.\n   }\n- Use #pragma omp for schedule(dynamic) nowait (but need barrier after loop). We'll use default(none) and share necessary variables.\n\nAfter loop, we merge:\n- int total = 0;\n- For t in 0..num_threads-1: for j in 0..k-1: if dist_local[t][j] != INFINITY (or sentinel), add to all array.\n- Sort all by distance then index.\n\nThen take first k labels into final_labels array, then compute most frequent as before.\n\nWe must free allocated memory.\n\nPotential performance: The merge step is O(num_threads * k log(num_threads*k)) which is small since k likely <= 100? Actually problem statement doesn't specify max k but typical KNN uses small k. Even if large, it's fine.\n\nNow write the code in src/knn.c with proper includes and OpenMP pragmas.\n\nAlso need to ensure that we use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic) correctly: The loop variable i is shared? We'll declare i private automatically.\n\nWe must be careful about using C99 features (declaring variables inside loops). Current code uses C89 style but it's fine with -std=c99 or default gcc. We'll keep consistent.\n\nNow produce the JSON response with hypothesis, transformations, sources_kept empty, files containing only src/knn.c content, and notes.\n\nHypothesis: \"Using OpenMP dynamic scheduling over groups with per-thread local top-k arrays storing distance, label, and global point index ensures load balance across variable-length groups while preserving exact sequential output order via a deterministic merge by distance then global insertion index.\"\n\nTransformations: [\"Added #include <omp.h>\", \"Precomputed cumulative group lengths for global indexing\", \"Parallelized outer loop over groups with schedule(dynamic)\", \"Each thread maintains private top-k arrays (distance, label, global_index)\", \"After parallel region, merged per-thread candidates into a single array sorted by distance then global index to select final k nearest neighbors\"]\n\nNotes: \"This approach guarantees correctness because the merge uses global point order as tie-breaker, matching sequential insertion behavior. Dynamic scheduling handles variable group sizes.\"\n\nNow write the file content.\n\nWe need to output complete contents of changed files with their paths. Only src/knn.c changes; knn.h unchanged but we must include it? The response requires only files that are modified. We'll provide only src/knn.c.\n\nLet's craft the new src/knn.c:\n\n```c\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#include <omp.h>\n#include <float.h>\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    // Precompute cumulative lengths for global indexing\n    int *cum = (int *)malloc((n_groups + 1) * sizeof(int));\n    cum[0] = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        cum[i+1] = cum[i] + groups[i].length;\n    }\n\n    int num_threads = omp_get_max_threads();\n    // Allocate per-thread local top-k arrays\n    float (*dist_local)[k] = (float(*)[k])malloc(num_threads * k * sizeof(float));\n    char (*lab_local)[k]  = (char(*)[k])malloc(num_threads * k * sizeof(char));\n    int   (*idx_local)[k] = (int(*)[k])malloc(num_threads * k * sizeof(int));\n\n    // Initialize all entries to sentinel values\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) num_threads(num_threads)\n    for (int t = 0; t < num_threads; t++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < k; j++) {\n            dist_local[t][j] = FLT_MAX;\n            lab_local[t][j] = -1;\n            idx_local[t][j] = -1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel default(none) shared(n_groups, groups, to_evaluate, k, cum, dist_local, lab_local, idx_local)\n    {\n        int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n\n        #pragma omp for schedule(dynamic)\n        for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n            Group g = groups[i];\n            float *dist_tid = dist_local[tid];\n            char  *lab_tid  = lab_local[tid];\n            int   *idx_tid  = idx_local[tid];\n\n            for (int j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\n                float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, g.points[j]);\n                int global_idx = cum[i] + j;\n\n                // Insert into local top-k list\n                int x;\n                for (x = 0; x < k; x++) {\n                    if (d < dist_tid[x]) break;\n                }\n                if (x < k) {\n                    // Shift elements right from x to k-2\n                    for (int y = k - 1; y > x; y--) {\n                        dist_tid[y] = dist_tid[y-1];\n                        lab_tid[y]  = lab_tid[y-1];\n                        idx_tid[y]  = idx_tid[y-1];\n                    }\n                    dist_tid[x] = d;\n                    lab_tid[x]  = g.label;\n                    idx_tid[x]  = global_idx;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Collect all candidates from per-thread arrays\n    int total_candidates = 0;\n    for (int t = 0; t < num_threads; t++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < k; j++) {\n            if (dist_local[t][j] != FLT_MAX) {\n                total_candidates++;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    typedef struct {\n        float dist;\n        char label;\n        int idx;\n    } Candidate;\n\n    Candidate *cands = (Candidate *)malloc(total_candidates * sizeof(Candidate));\n    int pos = 0;\n    for (int t = 0; t < num_threads; t++) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < k; j++) {\n            if (dist_local[t][j] != FLT_MAX) {\n                cands[pos].dist  = dist_local[t][j];\n                cands[pos].label = lab_local[t][j];\n                cands[pos].idx   = idx_local[t][j];\n                pos++;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Sort by distance then global index\n    qsort(cands, total_candidates, sizeof(Candidate), compare_candidates);\n\n    // Extract top-k labels (first k after sorting)\n    char *labels = (char *)malloc(k * sizeof(char));\n    for (int i = 0; i < k && i < total_candidates; i++) {\n        labels[i] = cands[i].label;\n    }\n    // If fewer than k candidates, fill remaining with -1? Not needed as problem guarantees enough points.\n\n    free(cands);\n    free(dist_local);\n    free(lab_local);\n    free(idx_local);\n    free(cum);\n\n    // Compute most frequent label (same as original)\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\n\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\n    int current_frequency = 1;\n\n    for (int i = 1; i < k; i++) {\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n            }\n            current_frequency = 1;\n        } else {\n            current_frequency++;\n        }\n\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n        }\n    }\n\n    free(labels);\n    return most_frequent;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\n    \n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\n    }\n\n    int k = parse_k();\n\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\n\n    printf(\"%c\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\n}\n```\n\nWe need a compare_candidates function. Add it before knn or inside? We'll add static helper.\n\nAlso note: The original code uses qsort on labels array; we still do that after extracting top-k labels.\n\nPotential issue: In the merge step, we sort all candidates and take first k. But if there are more than k candidates with same distance at boundary, sorting by index ensures correct selection of those with smallest indices (matching sequential). However, what if total_candidates < k? Then we have fewer than k labels; but problem likely guarantees enough points.\n\nWe must ensure that the compare function for qsort on Candidate uses dist then idx. We'll define:\n\n```c\nint compare_candidates(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    Candidate *ca = (Candidate *)a;\n    Candidate *cb = (Candidate *)b;\n    if (ca->dist < cb->dist) return -1;\n    if (ca->dist > cb->dist) return 1;\n    // distances equal: compare global index\n    if (ca->idx < cb->idx) return -1;\n    if (ca->idx > cb->idx) return 1;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nAdd this function before knn.\n\nAlso need to include <omp.h> and <float.h>.\n\nNow produce JSON."
